## Why This Is Asked
Probes privacy-preserving multi-tenant data architectures, lineage, and streaming trade-offs not covered by earlier questions.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Data contracts and schema drift
- Dynamic masking of PII
- GDPR delete and lineage preservation
- Iceberg lakehouse semantics
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: mask PII fields for non-admins
function maskPII(record, role) {
  if (role !== 'admin') {
    record.email = '***@***';
    record.phone = record.phone?.replace(/\\d/g, 'x');
  }
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant deletes across long-running streams?
- How would you test schema drift and masking policy changes in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multi-city data sovereignty, strict data contracts, and production readiness for evolving schemas at scale. It probes thinking on idempotent upserts, late-arriving data, GDPR deletion, and governance alongside trade-offs between Spark and Flink.

## Key Concepts
- City-level partitioning and data isolation
- Data contracts via schema registry and schema evolution
- Lakehouse upserts with Iceberg and idempotency guarantees
- Late data handling with watermarks and allowed lateness
- GDPR deletion via tombstones and per-record lineage
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs for latency and throughput
- Data-quality gates and contract testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo contract check for incoming event against schema registry
function validateEvent(event, schema) {
  // validate required fields and types; fail fast on mismatch
  // this is illustrative; integrate with a real registry in production
  if (!event.vehicle_id || !event.ts) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-city data masking while preserving analytics?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward/forward compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end data pipeline with per-record contracts, canonical schemas, late data handling, and basic data quality reconciliation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-record contracts
- Lightweight schema registry
- Canonical schema and partitioning
- Watermarking for late data
- Data reconciliation and basic validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight per-record contract check (pseudo)
function validate(record){
  if(!record) return false;
  const has=(k)=>record[k]!==undefined && record[k]!==null;
  return has('device_id') && has('event_ts') && has('metric');
}
```

